What:           /proc/priority_locks
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        QWEOVO kernel maintainer
Description:
                Runtime UID-wide static nice rules for Android application UIDs.
                Public read/write interface (0666); no root requirement.

                Read: TSV header uid<TAB>nice<TAB>updated_ns, one row per rule.
                updated_ns is a decimal boot-time nanosecond token; treat as
                a string. Rules are listed even with no running process.

                Write one ASCII command, at most 128 bytes including optional
                trailing whitespace/newline:
                  lock UID NICE
                  unlock UID

                UID is the full unsigned 32-bit UID; UID modulo 100000 must be
                10000..19999. NICE is -20..19. Maximum 256 rules. Isolated and
                system UIDs are excluded. No bulk-clear command is provided.
                Duplicate lock updates a rule; unlock is idempotent. Invalid
                input changes no rule (EINVAL/E2BIG); a full table yields ENOSPC.

                Lock applies to current and future SCHED_NORMAL/SCHED_BATCH
                threads through native nice and scheduler entry points, fork,
                and credential publication. Same UID shares one rule across
                processes/packages. Unlock removes the constraint and retains
                current nice values. Rules survive application restarts and
                are cleared by device reboot. No on-disk persistence.

                RT, deadline, idle policy, PI boosts, cgroups, CPU affinity and
                utilization clamps retain their native semantics. No new task
                structure fields or exported symbols are introduced.

                Discover with /proc/qweovo_info capability priority_uid_lock,
                interfaces.priority_locks and priority_lock_configuration
                api_version=1, scope=uid, supported_policies=[0,3]. Boot option
                qweovo_uid_nice_lock=0 disables enforcement and rejects writes
                with EOPNOTSUPP; capability reports false.
